<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for Recap. Applied by `codesign --options runtime
  --entitlements` to both Recap.app and the ffmpeg it bundles — see docs/RELEASE.md.

  Deliberately short. The hardened runtime is a set of *restrictions*; every
  entitlement here is a hole punched in it, and notarization review — plus anyone
  auditing the binary — reads each one as a claim we need it. So the rule applied
  below is: nothing goes in that hasn't got a concrete caller in this codebase.
  The equally important half of that is recorded at the bottom, where the
  entitlements Recap does *not* take are listed with the reason, because the
  default reflex when signing fails is to paste in the Electron set and move on.

  NOT App Sandbox. Recap uses `macos-private-api` for the transparent overlay
  window, which already rules out the Mac App Store (see Cargo.toml), and it
  spawns ffmpeg against arbitrary user-chosen output paths. Direct notarized
  distribution only, so the sandbox key is absent rather than empty.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Microphone. Audio narration is captured by the bundled ffmpeg via its
      avfoundation input, not by Recap itself, so this has to be granted to both
      binaries: TCC shows the consent prompt against the responsible process
      (Recap.app, which is why NSMicrophoneUsageDescription lives in Info.plist),
      but the entitlement check is per-signature and it is ffmpeg's signature
      that gets checked when ffmpeg opens the device. Sign the resource with the
      same entitlements file and both halves line up.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
<!--
  Deliberately absent — do not add these back without a concrete failure to point at:

  * com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit
    Only needed when JavaScript is JIT-compiled inside our own address space.
    Recap is WKWebView, and WebKit runs JS out-of-process in the system-owned
    com.apple.WebKit.WebContent, which carries its own entitlements. This is the
    single most cargo-culted key in Tauri entitlements files; it comes from
    Electron, which embeds V8 in-process and genuinely does need it.

  * com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory
    Same story, and strictly weaker protection than allow-jit. Nothing here
    generates code at runtime.

  * com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
    Would let us load code signed by another Team ID into our process. We load
    none: `otool -L vendor/ffmpeg` is system dylibs and system frameworks only,
    and ffmpeg is a *child process* anyway — library validation is a
    within-process rule, so spawning a differently-signed binary never trips it.
    If a user points RECAP_FFMPEG at a Homebrew ffmpeg, that dynamically linked
    mess loads into ffmpeg's process, not ours, and its own signature governs.

  * com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables
    Nothing sets DYLD_* on the children; ffmpeg::quiet_command passes the
    environment through untouched.

  * com.apple.security.automation.apple-events
    No osascript, no NSAppleScript, no Apple Events sent anywhere. Both
    subprocesses (bundled ffmpeg, Apple's /usr/sbin/screencapture) are spawned
    with posix_spawn through std::process::Command.

  * Screen Recording
    Has no entitlement to grant. It is pure TCC: the user enables Recap under
    System Settings -> Privacy & Security -> Screen Recording, keyed on the
    signed bundle identity. This is exactly why a stable Developer ID signature
    matters for release builds — an ad-hoc signature changes identity on every
    build and macOS re-prompts, or worse, silently keeps denying.

  * com.apple.security.get-task-allow
    Debugger attach. Must never be present in a distributed build; notarization
    rejects it outright. Absent here, and RELEASE.md verifies it stays absent.
-->
</plist>
